# Untitled

UV enhanced \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas sensing at room temperature based on coral-like tin diselenide/MOFs-derived nanoflower-like tin dioxide heteronanostructures
Tingting Li, Dongzhi Zhang, Qiannan Pan, Mingcong Tang, Sujing Yu
College of Control Science and Engineering, China University of Petroleum (East China), Qingdao 266580, China
## A R T I C L E  I N F O
Keywords: Tin selenide Hydrothermal \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) sensor UV illumination Heterostructure Metal-organic frameworks
## A B S T R A C T
In this paper, a coral-like tin diselenide/MOFs-derived flower-like tin dioxide \(\mathrm{(SnSe_{2}/S n O_{2})}\) nanocomposite film sensor was fabricated by hydrothermal method for \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas detection. Many characterization methods were used to examine the structural composition and micro-morphology of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\), and \(\mathrm{SnSe_{2}/S n O_{2}}\) samples. At room temperature, the \(\mathrm{SnSe_{2}/S n O_{2}}\) composite film sensor had a higher response to \(20\ \mathrm{ppm}\ \mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas when the mass ratio of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) was 1:2. A series of experiments were performed to investigate the \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) sensing properties of this sensor. The results of gas-sensing tests indicated that the \(\mathrm{SnSe_{2}/S n O_{2}}\) (1:2) nanocomposite sensor had excellent \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas sensitivity performance, better linearity and selectivity compared with pristine \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) film sensors. In particular, the response value of the \(\mathrm{SnSe_{2}/S n O_{2}}\) nanocomposite sensor towards \(20~\mathrm{ppm}\) \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas was obviously improved from 7.4 to 12.6 under UV illumination, and the response/recovery times towards \(10\,\mathrm{ppm}\,\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas were shortened from \(110\,\mathrm{s}/160\) to \(80\,\mathrm{s}/144\,\mathrm{s}\). The possible \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) sensing mechanism was related to the heterostructure between n-type \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\) and n-type \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) nanomaterials, and at the same time, we proposed that photoelectrons excited by UV light could improve the sensing characteristics of the \(\mathrm{SnSe_{2}/S n O_{2}}\) nanocomposite towards \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas.
## 1. Introduction
Nitrogen dioxide \(\left(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\right)\) is mainly from automobile exhaust and factory pollution, which can cause photochemical smog, respiratory infections and acid rain [1–4]. As \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas has serious harm to the natural environment and human health, many regions have issued relevant regulations on \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) emissions [5]. According to the American health safety standards, the threshold limit value (TLV) for \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas is \(3\,\mathrm{ppm}\) [6]. Meanwhile, \(\mathrm{NO}_{\mathrm{x}}\) can be used as a biomarker for the diagnosis of diseases such as gastrointestinal and pulmonary diseases [7]. Therefore, researches on high-sensitivity sensors of \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas with low detection limit for environmental detection and disease monitoring have become very urgent.
Recently, two-dimensional (2D) layered materials are widely used due to their high carrier mobility and unique structure. Among them, 2D transition metal dichalcogenides (TMDs) have become ideal materials for the preparation of electronic devices such as field effect transistors and photodetectors because of their unique electrical and optical properties [8–10]. They also have unique advantages in sensing application due to their strong gas adsorption and special planar crystal structure
Recently, two-dimensional (2D) layered materials are widely used due to their high carrier mobility and unique structure. Among them, 2D transition metal dichalcogenides (TMDs) have become ideal materials for the preparation of electronic devices such as field effect transistors and photodetectors because of their unique electrical and optical properties [8–10]. They also have unique advantages in sensing application due to their strong gas adsorption and special planar crystal structure [11]. For example, two-dimensional transition metal materials such as \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}\) [12,13], \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}\) [14,15], and \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\) [16,17] with different morphologies have been widely researched because of their good response to toxic gases. Among TMDs, \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\) has a large specific surface area, characteristics of high carrier mobility, and hexagonal structure. In recent years, it has begun to be used for gas detection research, such as \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) and \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas sensing [16,18–19]. Moreira et al. studied the gas sensitivity of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\) sensors to \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) and \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\), and the results showed that \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\) sensor had stable repeatability and long-term stability under UV light [19]. This proves the feasibility of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\) in the research of gas sensors. However, the original \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\) material has certain limitations, such as poor selectivity and low sensitivity, which affect the sensor's sensing performance. Previous reports indicated that the formation of heterojunctions could improve the sensing characteristics of gas sensors [20]. Zhang et al. prepared \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}\) p-n junction sensor by atomically stacking n-type and p-type thin \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}\) films [21]. The sensor showed excellent selectivity as well as response towards \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas at room temperature in comparison with the single p-type or n-type \(\mathrm{MoS}_{2}\) sensor. They also reported an outstanding room-temperature \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas sensor based on \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}\) nanosheets/carbon nanofibers composite. They indicated
that the composite sensor exhibited better gas sensing properties compared with the single sensing material, and the enhancement in sensing properties could be attributed to the exposure of \(\mathrm{WS}_{2}\) edges [18]. In addition, the ultraviolet (UV) illumination is also an effectively strategy to further improve the response while shorten response/r-ecovery times. Shao et al. constructed a room-temperature \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas sensor based on \(\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}/\mathrm{ZnO}\) yolk-shell nanofibers, which exhibited an improved response and shorter response/recovery times under UV activation [22]. They attributed the improved sensing performance to the participation of photo-generated charges and the positive effects of heterojunction. Zhang et al. prepared a flexible \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) sensor with outstanding response and good selectivity based on \(\mathrm{WSe}_{2}\) nanosheets under UV illumination [23]. The UV illumination exhibited significantly improvement in the gas sensing properties to oxidizing gas and no effect on reducing gases.
In recent years, metal oxide such as \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) [24,25], ZnO [26,27], \(\mathrm{Fe}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}\) [28,29], and \(\mathrm{Co}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}\) [30,31] have been widely used in sensing because of their advantages such as excellent selectivity, low cost, and easy preparation. The hot-research on metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) with high porosity and large specific surface area has been applied in various fields, such as super capacitors [32], photocatalysts [33] and sensors [34]. MOFs is a new type of porous material, and its organic ligands are decomposed by high-temperature calcination to form a metal oxide with a large surface area and more gas adsorption sites, which further improves the gas sensing performance. \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) derived from MOFs is an n-type semiconductor with a wide band gap of \(3.6\;\mathrm{eV}\), and mostly used to detect gases such as CO [25,35], \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) [36,37] and \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) [38,39]. Wu et al. prepared \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) NPs/RGO hydrogels and confirmed that this structure had excellent detection performance of \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) [40]. Bang et al. presented a porous \(\mathrm{Si}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) nanowires composite material, and exhibited good response towards \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\) gas [38]. Although \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) has many advantages, it needs to work at high temperatures, resulting in a high energy consumption and complex operations, which affects the use of sensors.
In this paper, we studied a room temperature \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas sensor based on \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) nanocomposite. The surface morphology and elemental compositions of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) composite made by hydrothermal methods were characterized. The prepared \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) film sensor with the optimum mass ratio had excellent selectivity and stability to \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas at room temperature. For performance comparison, we studied \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) sensors based on \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\). In addition, we explored the effect of ultraviolet visible light on the sensing characteristics of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) sensors. The potential gas-sensing mechanism and illumination mechanism of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) composite were further discussed from n-n heterostructure and light-excited electrons.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Materials
Hydrazine hydrate \(\left(\mathrm{N}_{2}\mathrm{H}_{4}{\cdot}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)\), selenium dioxide \(\left(\mathrm{SeO}_{2}\right)\), tin chloride dihydrate \(\left(\mathrm{SnCl}_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\right)\), tin sulfate \(\left(\mathrm{SnSO}_{4}\right)\), sodium hydroxide (NaOH) and terephthalic acid \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\right)\) were from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent.
## 2.2. Fabrication of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) nanocomposite
Coral tin diselenide \(\left(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\right)\) was prepared by a hydrothermal reduction method. Add \(0.01\ \mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{SnCl}_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and \(0.02\ \mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{SeO}_{2}\) successively into \(70\ \mathrm{mL}\) deionized (DI) water and stir for \(30\ \mathrm{min}\). Add hydrazine hydrate \(\left(10\ \mathrm{mL}\right)\) slowly to the mixed solution, and the solution quickly turned dark red with many small bubbles. The resulting solution was transferred to an autoclave with a capacity of \(100\ \mathrm{mL}\), and hydrothermally treated at \(180\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(24\ \mathrm{h}\). The solution was cooled to room temperature, and centrifuged and washed for 5–6 times, and finally dried to obtain black powder.
MOF-derived tin dioxide was prepared as follows. During preparation, \(0.012\ \mathrm{mol}\) terephthalic acid \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDC}\right)\) and \(0.024\ \mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{NaOH}\) were dissolved into \(30\ \mathrm{mL}\) deionized water with stirring. After stirring for \(10\ \mathrm{min}\), \(6\ \mathrm{mL}\ \mathrm{SnSO}_{4}\) aqueous solution \((0.25\ \mathrm{M})\) was uniformly dropped into the mixed solution, and then stirred for \(5\ \mathrm{h}\) to form a precipitate. The collected white product was washed with ethanol and DI water alternately, and the obtained product was dried at \(50\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) to form Sn-MOF powder. Finally, Sn-MOF was annealed in air at \(400\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) for \(2\ \mathrm{h}\) with a heating rate of \(2\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}\).
\(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) with mass ratios of 1:1, 1:2 and 3:1 were dispersed into deionized water by sonication for \(1\ \mathrm{h}\). The resulting suspension was spin-coated on a circular interdigital electrode based on \(\mathrm{Cu/Ni}\) metal material, and dried at \(60\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}\) in a vacuum environment.
## 2.3. Instrument and analysis
The crystal structures of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) nanocomposite were recorded using a X-ray diffractometer (XRD, Rigaku D/ Max 2500 PC, Japan) with \(\mathrm{Cu}\ \mathrm{K}\alpha\) radiation \((\lambda=1.5418\ \mathrm{\AA})\) in the range of \(10{-}80^{\circ}\). X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS, Thermo Scientific K-Alpha XPS spectrometer) was used to detect the chemical compositions of samples, and the morphologies of pristine \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) nanocomposite were observed by a scanning electron microscope (SEM, Hitachi S-4800). The surface morphology of the sample was examined by a transmission electron microscope (TEM, JEOL JEM-2100). Accelerated surface area and porosimetry system (Micromeritics, ASAP 2460) was used to examine the Brunner-Emmet-Teller surface areas and pore size distribution of the \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) nanocomposite.
All gas-sensing tests were carried out in a self-made test chamber at room temperature \((25\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})\) with a relative humidity (RH) of \(43\%\), and the test device is shown in Fig. 1. With the air as the background, the sensors were exposed to \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas with a concentration of \(1{-}20\ \mathrm{ppm}\), and sensors resistance signal was collected using keynight 34470A. The DC voltages of 3.2, 3.6 and \(4.4\ \mathrm{V}\) was used to power the UV light, the UV intensities of which were 64, 72 and \(90\ \mathrm{mW\ cm}^{-2}\), respectively. The sensor response (S) is expressed by \(\mathrm{S}=\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{g}}/\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{a}}\), in which \(\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{a}}\) and \(\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{g}}\) are the resistance base value in air and the resistance value in \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas, respectively. The other dry gases such as ammonia and formaldehyde used in our experiments were purchased from Dalian Date Gas Technology Co., Ltd. The response/recovery time can be defined as the time required for the initial resistance of the sensor to increase or decrease to \(90\%\) of the total resistance change during the absorption and desorption of the detected gas [41–43].
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characterization results
