The microstructure and crystallinity of the pristine \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) samples were further characterized by TEM technology, and the results are shown in Fig. 4. The very thin and irregular nanosheets can be seen in Fig. 4(a) and (d), which is consistent with the results of SEM observation, namely that the basic structure unit of the two single materials are nanosheets. The lattice spacings of 0.34 and \(0.26\;\mathrm{nm}\) in Fig. 4(c) can correspond to the (111) and (101) crystal faces of \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\), respectively [47]. The lattice spacing of \(0.30\;\mathrm{nm}\) in Fig. 4(f) can match well with the (101) crystal face of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\) [48]. Fig. 4(g-i) illustrate the low- and high-resolution TEM images of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) composite. As shown in Fig. 4(i), the lattice spacings related to \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\) can be
found, indicating the effective combination of the two materials.
The XPS is a measurement technique for detecting the chemical valence of materials and elemental compositions. Fig. 5(a) confirms the existence of three elements of Sn, Se, and O. The Sn 3d spectrum displayed in Fig. 5(b) has two characteristic peaks located at 486.75 and \(495.16~\mathrm{eV}\), respectively representing Sn \(3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}\) and Sn \(3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}\). There is an energy difference of \(8.41~\mathrm{eV}\) between Sn \(3\mathrm{d}_{5/2}\) and Sn \(3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}\), which can indicate the existence of \(\mathrm{Sn}^{4+}\) state. As shown in Fig. 5(c), the energy peaks located at 54.23 and \(55.17~\mathrm{eV}\) respectively correspond to the characteristic peaks of Se \(3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}\) and Se \(3\mathrm{d}_{3/2}\) [46]. Fig. 5(d) reveals the O 1s spectrum of the \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) composite. The peak of \(530.75~\mathrm{eV}\) in the O 1s spectrum corresponds to the lattice oxygen \(\left(\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{L}}\right)\), while the other characteristic peaks at 531.79 and \(532.54~\mathrm{eV}\) are respectively related to the oxygen vacancy \(\left(\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{V}}\right)\) and chemisorbed oxygen \(\left(\mathrm{O}_{\mathrm{C}}\right)\) of \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\). The abundant oxygen vacancies on the \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) surface can act as active sites of the adsorption and reaction of gas molecules, while large amounts of chemisorbed oxygen means the formation of many chemical adsorbed oxygen species on the surface, both of which are beneficial to the enhancement of gas sensing performance.
Fig. 6(a) illustrates the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) adsorption/desorption isotherm and the corresponding pore size distribution of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) composite. The composite is composed of coral-like \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\) and flower-like \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\), and they are in close contact to form mesoporous nanostructure. There is a hysteresis loop at relative pressure of 0.43–0.99, indicating that both macropores and mesopores exist in the \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) composite. The BET surface area of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) composite is \(42.35~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\), suggesting a large surface area. Its pore size distribution is around \(7.41~\mathrm{nm}\) based on the
Barrett-Joyner-Halenda (BJH) desorption isotherm analysis.
The current-voltage characteristic curves of pristine \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) composite were characterized using Keithley-2400 with the scanning voltage of \(-5\) to \(5\,\mathrm{V}\), and the results are shown in Fig. 6(b). As seen from the figure, the I-V characteristic curves of single \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\) are linear. The I-V characteristic curve of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) is nonlinear with a rectifying behavior. The forward current of the \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) composite is lower than the reverse current, indicating that there is a heterojunction structure between \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\).
## 3.2. \(N O_{2}\) sensing properties
Different mass ratios of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) have different response characteristics. To study the effect of material ratio on sensor sensitivity, a series of comparative experiments were performed. Fig. 7(a and b) show the resistance changes of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) film sensors with different ratios (1:1, 1:2, 3:1) of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) under different concentrations of \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas. As can be seen clearly from this figure, the initial resistance value gradually increases along with the increase in mass ratio of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\) in composite nanomaterial. Fig. 7(c) further illustrates their corresponding response changes. The \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) (1:2) film sensor exhibits highest response compared with the other two sensors. The fitting function curves of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) composite with different proportions are shown in Fig. 7(d), which can be expressed as \(Y=1.149+0.0807X\), \(Y=1.200+0.305X\), and \(Y=1.417+0.183X\), respectively. The fitting coefficient of the \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) (1:2) sensor is 0.987, indicating a good linear relationship. Therefore, the \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) (1:2) film sensor was
used for the subsequent investigation. The sensing performances of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) composite were compared with those of pristine \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\). Fig. 7(e) indicates the dynamic responses of pristine \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\), pristine \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) composite sensors to 1–20 ppm \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas at room temperature. The sensor switches back and forth between air and \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\), and the response value of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) composite to 20 ppm \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) is 7.4, which is much higher than those of pristine \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) sensors. Fig. 7(f) shows the fitting function curves of the three sensors. The limit of detection (LOD) of the \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) composite sensor for \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas are 1 ppm. The fitting functions of the three sensors based on pristine \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\), pristine \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) composite are respectively \(Y=1.180+0.0416X\), \(Y=1.115+0.067X\) and \(Y=1.200+0.305X\), explaining that there are good linear relationship between the response and gas concentration, and the \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) composite sensor has better sensing performance compared with the pristine \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) sensors.
Anti-interference ability is an important performance of the sensor. The selectivity of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) sensor was analyzed by comparing the response in 5 ppm \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) and other interference gases such as sulfur dioxide \(\left(\mathrm{SO}_{2}\right)\), carbon dioxide \(\left(\mathrm{CO}_{2}\right)\), ammonia \(\left(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\right)\), acetone \(\left(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{COCH}_{3}\right)\), formaldehyde (HCHO) and methanol \(\left(\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{OH}\right)\). Fig. 8(a) illustrates the excellent selectivity of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) sensor to \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas compared with other interfering gases. The outstanding selectivity of the sensor towards \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas may be contributed to the high electron affinity \((2.3\;\mathrm{eV})\) of \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas molecules, which can easily capture electrons from the material surface to form negative ion \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{-}\) [49]. Repeatability is the key to whether the gas sensor can be applied [50]. The repeatability of the \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) sensor was tested in 3 and 5 ppm \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas for several times, which shows good repeatability (Fig. 8(b)). The long-term stability of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) sensor in 5, 10 and 20 ppm \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas at room temperature was further investigated. As shown in Fig. 8(c), the response values of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) sensor to the three different concentrations of \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\)
gas have not changed significantly within 30 days, thereby confirming excellent stability.
As reported, the water vapor usually interferes with the detection of target gas because of the competitive adsorption of pre-adsorbed oxygen species between water vapor and detected gas at a low working temperature [51]. Therefore, it is very necessary to investigate the humidity dependence of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) composite to \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas at room temperature. The actual dynamic resistance curves of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) (1:2) composite in
different relative humidity are shown in Fig. S1. Fig. 8(d) shows the corresponding relationship between the response and relative humidity. The response values of the \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) composite sensor to 5 ppm \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas at \(43\%\) RH and \(85\%\) RH are 2.75 and 1.9, respectively. The sensor response at \(85\%\) RH is \(30.9\%\) lower than that at \(43\%\) RH. Therefore, the subsequent research work can further explore ways to reduce the influence of humidity on gas sensing performance.
In recent years, in order to improve aerodynamics at room
temperature, various solutions have been proposed, for example irradiating sensing device with UV light [52]. The optical switch test of the \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) composite sensor was performed in air environment, and the interval of light switch was one minute. Fig. 9(a) shows the resistance measurement during the optical switch of the device. The resistance of the \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) sensor decreases significantly when the UV lamp is on. This phenomenon can be explained by the fact that UV light stimulates the generation of electron-hole pairs, increasing the conductivity of the material [53]. At the same time, the effect of UV intensity on the response at room temperature was also investigated, the results shown in Fig. 9(b and c). Obviously, the UV intensity has an obvious influence on the sensor response. The response exhibits a trend of first increasing and then decreasing, and the highest response occurs at the UV intensity of \(72\;\mathrm{mW}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\), which is contributed to the stronger bond between sensing material surface and \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas molecules. Fig. 9(d) shows the resistance change of the \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) sensor in different concentrations of \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas under UV illumination.
It can be seen from the response comparison in Fig. 10(a) that the UV light has a great influence on improving the sensing characteristics of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) sensor, and the response increases significantly after UV illumination. The response values of the \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) composite to 5, 10 and \(20\;\mathrm{ppm}\;\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas under UV light are 5.45, 9.53 and 12.6, respectively, which are higher than those without UV illumination. It is worth noting that compared with the sensor response without UV illumination, the UV illumination can effectively improve the response characteristics of the sensing device. Fig. 10(b) shows the correlation function curve of the sensor response and \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) concentration after UV irradiation. The fitting functions without and under UV irradiation can be respectively expressed as \(Y=1.200+0.305X\) and \(Y=1.797+0.590X\). Fig. 10(c) shows the time-response curves of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) sensors in 4 and \(10\;\mathrm{ppm}\;\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas. As shown, the response and recovery times of the \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) sensor in \(10\;\mathrm{ppm}\;\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) are 110 and \(160\;\mathrm{s}\), respectively, faster than those of pristine \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\) sensors. At the same time, Fig. 10(d) shows the response/recovery curves of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) sensor
under different lighting conditions. The response/recovery time of the sensor under UV light to \(10\;\mathrm{ppm}\;\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas is \(80/144\;\mathrm{s}\), which further improves the response characteristics. In addition, Table 1 compares our current work with the previously reported \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) sensing materials in terms of operating temperature, response and response/recovery times, which indicates that our \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas sensor has higher response and shorter response/recovery times at room temperature under UV irradiation.
## 3.3. \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) sensing mechanism
The main mechanism of the resistive semiconductor sensor can be explained by the resistance change of the sensing film, and this change mainly occurs in the process of gas adsorption and desorption. Both \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) are n-type semiconductors, most carriers of which are electrons, but their sensing mechanisms are different. For \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) sensors, the adsorbed \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) gas molecules on the surfaces of \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) NFs generate \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}\)ions by capturing electrons from the conduction band in air. When the sensing material is placed in \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) atmosphere, the gas molecules obtain electrons to form \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{-}\). In addition, it also reacts with \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}\)on material surface, which increases the resistance of \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) sensors. When it is placed in air again, \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) molecule releases the trapped electrons to conduction band layer of \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\), so that the sensor resistance is restored as before, and the specific reaction process is illustrated as follows [60]:
\[
\begin{array}{rl}&{\mathrm{O}_2\mathrm{(gas)}\rightarrow\mathrm{O}_2\mathrm{(ads)}}\\&{\mathrm{O}_2\mathrm{(ads)}+\mathrm{e}^-\rightarrow\mathrm{O}_2^-(\mathrm{ads})}\\&{\mathrm{NO}_2\mathrm{(gas)}+\mathrm{e}^-\rightarrow\mathrm{NO}_2^-(\mathrm{ads})}\\&{\mathrm{NO}_2\mathrm{(gas)}+\mathrm{O}_2^-(\mathrm{ads})+2\mathrm{e}^-\rightarrow\mathrm{NO}_2^-(\mathrm{ads})+2\mathrm{O}^-(\mathrm{ads})}\end{array}
\]
When the \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\) thin film sensor is placed in \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) atmosphere, \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) acquires electrons and becomes \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}^{-}\), without reacting with oxygen molecules because of the low adsorption energy of \(\mathrm{O}_{2}\) on \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\). Compared with pristine \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) films, it is found that sensing
characteristics of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) sensor shows an improved response at room temperature. The reasons why the \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) composite sensor responds higher than the single sensors are as follows. Firstly, the improvement in sensing performance is caused by the formation of n-n heterojunction between \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\). Fig. 11 (a and b) reveal the sensing mechanism of \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) sensing on \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) composite. The band gaps of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) are 1.39 and \(3.6\;\mathrm{eV}\), the work functions are 5.3 and \(4.9\;\mathrm{eV}\), and the Fermi level of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\) is higher [61–67]. When the two materials are in contact with each other, the electrons flow from \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\) to \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) until the Fermi level reaches equilibrium. In this process, the energy band is bent at the contact interface between \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\), and a depletion layer is formed on the contact surface of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\) and the accumulation layer is formed on that of \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\), forming n-n heterojunction. When \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) composite film is placed in \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) environment, \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) reacts with \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}\)on the film surface, resulting in a decrease in carrier concentration, a widening in depletion layer, and an increase in resistance, thereby increasing the sensor response [68]. In addition, the \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) composite has a larger specific surface area because of its special structures, providing more active centers, which is conducive to
gas adsorption and desorption. At the same time, the nanoflower formed by many nanosheets is more conducive to the diffusion of \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) molecules.
When the ultraviolet rays irradiate the \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) composite thin film sensor, a large number of photo-generated electron pairs are generated, and the illumination changes the number of carriers on the surface by exciting photoelectrons flow from valence band of the material to conduction band, which leads to a decrease in sensor resistance [69]. Fig. 11(c and d) reveal the gas sensing mechanism and illumination mechanism of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) composite by n-n type heterostructure and light-excited electrons. Due to the presence of photoelectrons, the adsorbed oxygen molecules are transformed into atomic oxygen ions \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}\) (hv), as specifically reflected below [70]:
\[
\mathrm{O}_2(\mathrm{ads})+\mathrm{e}^-(\mathrm{hv})\rightarrow\mathrm{O}_2^-(\mathrm{hv})
\]
When the sensor is exposed to \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) atmosphere, some gas molecules react with \(\mathrm{O}_{2}^{-}\) (hv) on the surface of sensing film, while some react directly with the excited electrons \(\mathrm{e}^{-}\) (hv) and adsorb on the sensing surface. As each reaction consumes electrons, the resistance of the
resistive layer increases until it stabilizes. Due to the participation of excited electrons generated by the UV light, the gas sensing response increases significantly, and the above reaction is shown below:
\[
\begin{array}{rl}&{\mathrm{NO}_2(\mathrm{gas})+\mathrm{e}^-(\mathrm{hv})\rightarrow\mathrm{NO}_2^-(\mathrm{ads})}\\&{\mathrm{NO}_2(\mathrm{gas})+\mathrm{O}_2^-(\mathrm{hv})+2\mathrm{e}^-(\mathrm{hv})\rightarrow\mathrm{NO}_2^-(\mathrm{ads})+2\mathrm{O}^-(\mathrm{ads})}\end{array}
\]
## 4. Conclusions
A coral-like tin diselenide/MOFs-derived flower-like tin dioxide composite thin film sensor was successfully synthesized by a hydrothermal method. The composite films were characterized by XRD, SEM and XPS. At room temperature, the \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) composite film sensor has a higher response to \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas when the mass ratio of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) was 1:2. A series of comparative experiments had proved the excellent sensing performances of \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) sensor, and simultaneously confirmed that UV light could further improve the gas sensing properties of composite films. The potential gas sensing mechanism and illumination mechanism of the \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) composite film was explained from n-n type heterostructure and light-excited electrons. This work finally proves that the \(\mathrm{SnSe}_{2}/\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) composite is a promising candidate for monitoring \(\mathrm{NO}_{2}\) gas at room temperature.
